00:12:54.125 --> 00:12:55.095
Like that's an awesome mission, right?

00:12:55.095 --> 00:12:57.495
And so there's, when you think about
a company there's like the mission,

00:12:57.495 --> 00:12:58.295
there's the vision.

00:12:58.295 --> 00:13:00.755
Like when we achieve the mission
here's what the world will look like.

00:13:00.755 --> 00:13:01.625
There's the strategy,

00:13:01.625 --> 00:13:04.295
which is like, here's how we're going to,
in the fewest number of words,

00:13:04.295 --> 00:13:07.075
how we're going to go about achieving our
mission, and then there's the tactics.

00:13:07.075 --> 00:13:09.620
There's the things that
you do week over week in

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:12.600
pursuit of your strategy to validate
whether that strategy is true or not.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:15.750
I think the advice that Adora's
giving is more like strategic advise.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:18.490
Where it's like okay, great, that's
an awesome mission but where do you start?

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:19.900
Like what is the first
value that you create for

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:22.730
people and then that lets you create that
second value, and the third value, and

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:25.320
then you've created all of the value for
that population of people.

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:26.280
But where do you start?

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah, if you think about if
you're going to apply to college,

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.410
there's many steps in that and
you can't tackle every single step.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:35.300
So, find the one in which people
are having the most issue with or

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:37.850
they need the most help and
which they will actually pay for as well,

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:39.975
because you're trying, obviously,
build a business, right?

00:13:39.975 --> 00:13:42.110
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; And, it's not just product strategy.

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:46.190
There's also the go to market strategy,
which is, that's a lot of people, right?

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:50.020
How many people, is it like 50% of
people even that take the SAT in the US?

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:50.640
Right?
It's like half

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:52.020
the people don't even take the SAT, right?

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:55.400
So you're talking about large numbers
of people at every step of the funnel.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:58.640
And so who you target is also part of your
strategy, and who you target is going to

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:02.850
be directly tied to what you build for
those people as the first step.

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:07.841
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that's a great point and
when we first started there are like

00:14:07.841 --> 00:14:12.834
many different angles we can target,
and we want to use the users' data and

00:14:12.834 --> 00:14:16.896
we want to just ask the users to
drive us to which is the focus.

00:14:16.896 --> 00:14:20.717
And I felt like because the early
acquisition is really hard for

00:14:20.717 --> 00:14:25.693
the users so I don't have enough people to
actually give me feedback on which areas

00:14:25.693 --> 00:14:27.080
they want to focus on.

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:30.090
Some early indication is
school selection and essay.

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:34.039
So these are the two so far that I can
identify from major is a science, math,

00:14:34.039 --> 00:14:35.040
and business.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.190
So those are three majors.

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:40.930
So we have some pieces of signals
that tell us which way we should go.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:43.270
But I know, in my heart,

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:47.060
that going forward there is some,
one area that I need to focus on.

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:49.330
I feel like we don't have
enough data to get there yet.

00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:52.550
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, the challenge of data,
this goes back to

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:57.480
there is two ways to figure
out what you want to build,

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:00.720
whether it's your go to market plan,
your product is the top down.

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:01.810
Which is you go get a bunch of data.

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.100
Where are the opportunities?

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:03.990
How do you slice the market?

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:05.530
We're going to start here and
then we're going to go here and

00:15:05.530 --> 00:15:06.190
then we're going to go here.

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:08.270
Or bottoms up which is you build
the thing, you put it out there,

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:08.970
people start using it.

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:09.820
They're like we want this.

00:15:09.820 --> 00:15:11.320
We move forward.

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:13.730
And a lot of software actually bottoms up,
is great.

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:17.540
Because the early adopters indicate where
the world is headed as we build for

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:19.640
them and then the rest of
the world kind of catches up.

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:23.660
It's like social networking,
obviously is a great example of that.

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:26.970
The challenge is if the people that are
the early adopters of your product are not

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:29.270
representative of what
everybody else is going to do,

00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:32.760
then you end up building a thing that
doesn't actually work for everybody else.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:35.080
And that's, I think, part of the challenge
that you would have right now if you do

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:37.725
that is, I suspect the people that
are coming to you are not going to be

00:15:37.725 --> 00:15:39.150
100% representative.

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:39.720
To me it sounds like

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:41.450
two different populations of
people that you're talking about.

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:44.600
The people who don't know about financial
aid and maybe are not applying to colleges

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:48.850
and not doing all this kind of stuff and
don't have the support, versus like

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:51.980
people who are organically finding
this and care about this stuff a lot.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:55.490
Like to me it actually sounds like
different populations of people.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:59.160
&gt;&gt; Okay, I think we have to finish up,
but just one last thing It seems

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:02.060
like a lot of people are coming to you for
advice anyway.

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:06.500
I might just start from there, and
just sit down with all these people And

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:10.110
just do it all day every single day for
the next, I don't know, few weeks.

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:14.570
And then, see if they're anything common
things that people are asking you.

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:17.630
Also, you have access to now 60 advisers.

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:20.050
You could also sit with them and
do the exact same thing.

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:22.580
It's totally not a scalable
thing to do in the future.

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:26.713
But, it seems like to figure out where
to start that's like one way to go about

00:16:26.713 --> 00:16:27.294
doing it.

00:16:27.294 --> 00:16:28.755
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:16:28.755 --> 00:16:29.441
Thank you.

00:16:29.441 --> 00:16:38.829
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Hey.

00:16:38.829 --> 00:16:39.594
&gt;&gt; Have a seat.

00:16:39.594 --> 00:16:40.894
&gt;&gt; Hi.
Adora?

00:16:40.894 --> 00:16:42.756
&gt;&gt; Good to meet you.

00:16:42.756 --> 00:16:46.251
&gt;&gt; Cool thanks for coming.

00:16:46.251 --> 00:16:48.010
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
&gt;&gt; What are you guys working on?

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:51.876
&gt;&gt; So I'm Sid and I'm the CEO and
co-founder of Yembo.

00:16:51.876 --> 00:16:55.859
At Yembo, we are bringing computer
vision technology to solve the price and

00:16:55.859 --> 00:16:58.786
cost estimation problem for
the home services market,

00:16:58.786 --> 00:17:01.789
specifically we are starting
with the moving industry.

00:17:01.789 --> 00:17:03.889
So let's say you are consumer and
you want to move, and

00:17:03.889 --> 00:17:05.870
you want to know how
much it's going to cost.

00:17:05.870 --> 00:17:09.398
You take a video and our technology
will identify all the items, and then,

00:17:09.398 --> 00:17:11.640
you get an upfront and accurate quote.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:16.130
So this is much better than
calling multiple moving companies,

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:20.020
staying on the phone for about half an
hour each to get arbitrary ground codes,

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:23.860
or waiting at home for
on site estimator to come visit.

00:17:23.860 --> 00:17:26.260
&gt;&gt; So is this an app?

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:27.675
&gt;&gt; So we-
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:17:27.675 --> 00:17:30.920
And we have multiple channels,
we did an NVP pilot with an app.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:33.514
We've also been working on just
having it directly on the web page of

00:17:33.514 --> 00:17:34.342
the moving company.

00:17:34.342 --> 00:17:38.896
&gt;&gt; So say, I'm at home, it sounds like I
take the app and I'm taking pictures and

00:17:38.896 --> 00:17:43.174
I'm sending it to you guys and then,
you kind of give me an estimate ASAP,

00:17:43.174 --> 00:17:44.508
essentially, okay.

00:17:44.508 --> 00:17:45.119
&gt;&gt; Correct, yeah,

00:17:45.119 --> 00:17:47.765
and we also have a solution where
you can text it to a number.

00:17:47.765 --> 00:17:48.600
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:49.253
&gt;&gt; But it's the same technology.

00:17:49.253 --> 00:17:51.939
&gt;&gt; Okay, and
this is more accurate than the, or

00:17:51.939 --> 00:17:56.400
what's the percent of accuracy do
you get versus someone coming in?

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:58.920
&gt;&gt; So
we are targeting the same accuracies.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:03.548
So the way we are thinking about it
is today the estimation process takes

00:18:03.548 --> 00:18:07.558
about a week for you to get a quote or
you get multiple quotes and

00:18:07.558 --> 00:18:11.760
you don't really understand
why a quote is what it is.

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:15.950
So our goal is to give you a complete
breakdown on what it is, and we still have

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:21.420
some human labor in the background,
cleaning up the mistakes the AI is making.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:25.240
So our goal is to reduce the human
time to less than five minutes.

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:27.522
&gt;&gt; Why is it so expensive,
or why is it so,

00:18:27.522 --> 00:18:31.135
take a week to get a quote like
where moving take coming along?

00:18:31.135 --> 00:18:36.820
&gt;&gt; So one main reason is if you are
scheduling an onsite estimator with it.

00:18:36.820 --> 00:18:41.513
So it costs the moving company a lot,
more than $200 for a visit.

00:18:41.513 --> 00:18:44.980
And you have to schedule
someone to come visit.

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:47.423
And these guys are able to
do 2 to 3 estimates a day.

00:18:47.423 --> 00:18:52.774
So in summertime and it's busy,
it gets really backed up.

00:18:52.774 --> 00:18:56.426
So most companies don't end up actually
sending an on-site estimator, and

00:18:56.426 --> 00:18:58.295
they give price quotes over the phone.

00:18:58.295 --> 00:19:02.570
Our research shows that 90% of this was
wrong, so consumers shop around and

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:08.860
that they don't really understand why
a companies quoting what they're quoting.

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:11.770
And in a lot of cases what happens
is there are these predatory

00:19:11.770 --> 00:19:15.930
providers in the market who take
advantage of this price opaqueness and

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:18.550
they give you a low ball quote,
get your business, and

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:22.640
on the day of your move specifically,
they slap you with last minute fee hikes.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:25.570
So we want to eliminate all of that.

00:19:25.570 --> 00:19:28.980
&gt;&gt; So once you get the quote are you
also doing the moving yourself or

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:32.000
are you selling leads?

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:33.240
&gt;&gt; So, our strategies,

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:36.880
we're working with moving companies
to do the estimating for them.

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:39.750
So we are not a moving company, and
our customer is the moving company.

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:41.064
&gt;&gt; So you're a white label solution for
them.

00:19:41.064 --> 00:19:41.738
&gt;&gt; Correct, yes.

00:19:41.738 --> 00:19:42.830
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:44.090
&gt;&gt; Why do you need computer vision for
this?

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:47.480
It seems like everything you described
to me sounds like process failures,

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:51.140
like transparency in a market place
failures, like it sounds like a bunch of,

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:52.580
well what does the computer
vision give you?

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:56.940
&gt;&gt; So our initial MVP that we deployed
doesn't have any computer vision.

00:19:56.940 --> 00:19:58.270
People just take videos and

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:00.820
someone watches it in
the back end to give a quote.

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:04.270
That takes around 90 minutes per person so
and

00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:07.650
also you need to hire and
scale human workforce to do that.

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:10.250
And there are a few other
companies that's doing this.

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:15.970
So our goal is, we eliminate like
majority of the work through using CV

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:22.130
and keep the human workload
to as less possible.

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:27.080
And from a product perspective
what we want to be also able

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:31.470
to deliver to consumers is a interactively
of them customizing their quote.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:36.210
So let's say you have a $2,000 budget and
everything,

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:38.430
moving everything is maybe
going to cost $3,000.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:41.730
Then you can decide, pick and
choose how you want to spend that money.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:45.120
&gt;&gt; How do you intend to charge for this?

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:47.253
And do you have current
customers right now?

00:20:47.253 --> 00:20:50.291
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we signed our first deal for

00:20:50.291 --> 00:20:54.480
a paid trial with a moving
company in San Diego.

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:59.175
There, we are doing this GPM solution
where it is $10 with no AI and

00:20:59.175 --> 00:21:04.000
$50 with AI and
a we are talking to multiple

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:09.510
large growing companies from $20 million
annual revenue to a few billion dollars.

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:13.370
And we are in the final stages of
negotiations with some of them

00:21:13.370 --> 00:21:17.940
on the timelines as well as
the features of a paid pilot.

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:20.350
So it's like enterprise level
contracts that we're going after.

00:21:21.700 --> 00:21:23.630
So what does success look like for you?

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:28.430
Is it accuracy or
what are these folks looking from you?

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:30.340
&gt;&gt; So, two different things.

00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:34.610
From a consumer standpoint,
they want to reduce the friction of,

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:39.320
they want to provide a better quality
of service because there's no way for

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:41.560
the good companies to
differentiate themselves right now.

00:21:41.560 --> 00:21:46.080
So they are looking at it as
a better way to do customer service.

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:48.490
And also from a cost stand point,

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:53.670
since it costs more than $200 to send
an on-site estimator and it doesn't scale,

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:57.360
they're looking at ways to increase
their bottom line and their profits.

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:02.678
&gt;&gt; I wonder if there's a bigger
market in you being the initial,

00:22:02.678 --> 00:22:08.813
people thinking you're the initial moving
company, or and you are selling the leads.

00:22:08.813 --> 00:22:11.445
I don't know if you want to get
into the actual moving itself,

00:22:11.445 --> 00:22:12.744
that seems quite different.

00:22:12.744 --> 00:22:15.936
Just operationally a headache,
but Instead,

00:22:15.936 --> 00:22:19.526
part of what you're doing
is also acquiring users and

00:22:19.526 --> 00:22:24.100
getting that sales process going and
making it a great experience.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:28.840
And if you do that really well, then,
why not just own the entire process?

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:29.980
The entire sale process, I guess.

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:32.125
&gt;&gt; That's something that we've considered,
but

00:22:32.125 --> 00:22:37.520
moving has a lot more,
I guess intricacies and just the code.

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:42.620
So right now, we're just hyperfocused
on generating the best code possible.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:45.480
That's something that we could
possibly do in the future.

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:48.170
And the second point is we're
not acquiring customers today.

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:50.450
The moving companies acquire
customers themselves.

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:54.230
So we are a technology that it's
either there on their website or

00:22:54.230 --> 00:22:56.960
they direct consumers to our app.

00:22:56.960 --> 00:23:01.109
&gt;&gt; Why do you charge more for
the AI and less for the people?

00:23:01.109 --> 00:23:03.072
&gt;&gt; So with the $10 solution,

00:23:03.072 --> 00:23:07.861
the assumption is if you're the moving
company, you watch the video and

00:23:07.861 --> 00:23:11.770
provide the coach yourself so
&gt;&gt; So there's some human,

00:23:11.770 --> 00:23:14.730
if you're the moving company, you have to
hire people to actually watch the video.

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:16.328
&gt;&gt; So
you're not hiring the people to do it.

00:23:16.328 --> 00:23:18.060
&gt;&gt; On the $10, no.

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:18.710
&gt;&gt; I see.

00:23:18.710 --> 00:23:22.150
&gt;&gt; Who is actually,
taking the non AI version?

00:23:23.620 --> 00:23:25.810
&gt;&gt; Right now,
our AI is not completely ready yet.

00:23:25.810 --> 00:23:29.310
So that's the product that
we are selling right now.

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:31.386
&gt;&gt; I see, but why not sell just the AI?

00:23:31.386 --> 00:23:32.561
I mean, that's what you're going to be.

00:23:32.561 --> 00:23:34.680
So why not sell the AI product now?

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:37.830
And you could still have
yourselves in the back.

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:39.104
Yeah, fake it 'til you make it, you know?

00:23:39.104 --> 00:23:41.523
&gt;&gt; So that's a good question.

00:23:41.523 --> 00:23:42.500
So two reasons.

00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:48.935
The first one is do you want to train
RAI on real world people's homes?

00:23:48.935 --> 00:23:52.229
You can imagine when you're moving your
house looks nothing like the beautiful

00:23:52.229 --> 00:23:54.189
catalog pictures-
&gt;&gt; It seems like this is,

00:23:54.189 --> 00:23:55.479
you need a lot of data, and

00:23:55.479 --> 00:23:59.800
to get a lot of data really quickly, You
could just provide this blanket solution,

00:23:59.800 --> 00:24:02.738
and all the moving companies
think they're getting this thing.

00:24:02.738 --> 00:24:03.534
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and so-
&gt;&gt; And

00:24:03.534 --> 00:24:05.840
you're providing the same amount
of quality either way, right?

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:11.020
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we had actually hiring
annotators that are outsourced,

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:14.300
where as our AI is improving
their workload is reducing, so

00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:15.300
that's something that we're doing.

00:24:16.550 --> 00:24:20.992
&gt;&gt; So homes are very different, and which
is why it's probably such a big problem,

00:24:20.992 --> 00:24:24.470
no one has figured out how to
standardize the quoting process.

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:29.295
How much data do you think you need before
you've gotten something that can just

00:24:29.295 --> 00:24:30.450
run on its own?

00:24:30.450 --> 00:24:35.548
&gt;&gt; So we are actively also web
scraping for data from Google Flickr,

00:24:35.548 --> 00:24:40.100
so we have a few million
submissions already downloaded.

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:45.485
So the goal, again, with the AI is the
accuracy keeps increasing with time and

00:24:45.485 --> 00:24:47.450
as more consumers use it.

00:24:47.450 --> 00:24:52.400
And we have a fail-safe
mechanism of humans filling

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:53.550
in the gaps in the mean time.

00:24:55.390 --> 00:24:57.780
&gt;&gt; What do you think your biggest or
what is your biggest challenge right now?

00:24:57.780 --> 00:24:59.591
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's a good question, so and

00:24:59.591 --> 00:25:03.000
it's one thing I think startup school
has actually helped us out with.

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:06.576
So we are going after large
enterprise contracts, and

00:25:06.576 --> 00:25:10.320
it's taking some time,
because our AI is not ready yet.

